      "problem": "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x-1}{x+3}.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2x-1}{x+3}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2y-1}{y+3}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2y-1}{y+3} \\\\ x(y+3) & =2y-1 \\\\ xy+3x & =2y-1 \\\\ 3x+1 & =2y-xy \\\\ 3x+1 & =y(2-x) \\\\ y & =\\frac{3x+1}{2-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3x+1}{2-x}.\n$$",
